  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B058",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B018",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B018 to B058. Optical lattice clocks recognize a sharply defined physical architecture: magic-wavelength confinement makes it possible to interrogate large atomic ensembles while controlling the trap-induced perturbation of the clock transition. The achievement has passed the relevant maturity test through independent reproduction and sustained frontier precision measurement. Katori's founding concept and Ye's pioneering realization give an unusually compact, intelligible attribution. This is a prize for the established atomic-physics and metrological advance, not for prospective redefinition of the second or other future applications.",
    "case_against": "B058 is still an instrument-centered achievement, and its later records and applications belong to a broad community rather than to two individuals. The historical review must therefore delimit Katori and Ye's founding contributions from subsequent performance advances. B018 remains scientifically profound, but its credit boundary is less cleanly separable from the still evolving superconducting-qubit enterprise.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that optical lattice clocks combine a discrete magic-wavelength architecture, reproducible frontier performance, and clear attribution to Katori and Ye.",
        "response": "I agree. In particular, the discussion has clarified that the physical achievement is not simply improved clock accuracy: it is the controllable lattice-clock regime itself. Its independent reproduction supplies the maturity evidence, while the narrow citation prevents later community developments from being absorbed into the award."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that a prize need not await eventual time-standard deployment because the lattice-clock principle, realization, and use as a probe of fundamental physics are already mature.",
        "response": "I agree with this distinction. Requiring a completed practical transition would confuse downstream deployment with the discovery. The present record is sufficient because the founding architecture is experimentally established; the citation should make no claim to later performance records or an eventual redefinition of the second."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson holds that aberration-corrected electron optics is a long-established observational breakthrough but identifies unresolved historical allocation among concepts, engineering, and parallel implementations.",
        "response": "B035 remains a strong alternative precisely because it opened a durable observational capability. I now place B058 ahead because its physical principle and proposed two-person attribution are more tightly bounded, subject to the same necessary archival discipline about foundational rather than later work."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone capture the founding concept and decisive realization without omitting an equivalently indispensable early contributor.",
      "How narrowly the citation must be framed to exclude later lattice-clock performance improvements and broader metrological developments."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My Chalmers and WACQT roles create a field-level connection to the superconducting-quantum-circuit community relevant to B018. I have changed my preferred configuration to B058; no candidate-specific conflict concerning Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
